articles.content_en:
Article 1: Where a judgment debtor fails to perform the obligations determined in an effective legal instrument and falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall include it in the list of judgment debtors subject to credit punishment and impose credit punishment upon it according to law:
(1) where it has the capacity to perform but refuses to perform the obligations determined in the effective legal instrument;
(2) where it obstructs or resists enforcement by means of forging evidence, violence, or threats;
(3) where it evades enforcement by means of false litigation, false arbitration, or concealing or transferring property;
(4) where it violates the property reporting system;
(5) where it violates a consumption restriction order;
(6) where it refuses, without justifiable reasons, to perform an enforcement settlement agreement.

Article 3: Where any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court shall not, pursuant to the provisions of Item (1) of Article 1 of these Provisions, include the judgment debtor in the list of judgment debtors subject to credit punishment:
(1) where it has provided sufficient and valid security;
(2) where the property already subject to measures such as sealing, seizure, and freezing is sufficient to satisfy the debt determined in the effective legal instrument;
(3) where the judgment debtor is later in the sequence of performance and, according to law, compulsory execution should not be applied to it;
(4) other circumstances that do not fall within the circumstance of having the capacity to perform but refusing to perform the obligations determined in an effective legal instrument.

Article 6: The information on the list of judgment debtors subject to credit punishment that is recorded and published shall include:
(1) where the judgment debtor is a legal person or other organization, its name, unified social credit code (or organization code), and the name of its legal representative or responsible person;
(2) where the judgment debtor is a natural person, his or her name, gender, age, and identity card number;
(3) the obligations determined in the effective legal instrument and the performance status of the judgment debtor;
(4) the specific circumstances of the judgment debtor's dishonest conduct;
(5) the issuing entity and document number of the enforcement basis, the enforcement case number, the case-filing time, and the enforcement court;
(6) other matters that the People's Court considers should be recorded and published and that do not involve state secrets, trade secrets, or personal privacy.

Article 10: Where any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court shall delete the dishonest information within three working days:
(1) the judgment debtor has performed the obligations determined in the effective legal instrument, or the People's Court has completed enforcement;
(2) the parties have reached an enforcement settlement agreement and have fully performed it;
(3) the applicant for enforcement has applied in writing to delete the dishonest information, and the People's Court has approved the application upon review;
(4) after the termination of the current enforcement procedure, inquiry into the judgment debtor's property has been conducted through the online enforcement investigation and control system two or more times, no property available for enforcement has been found, and neither the applicant for enforcement nor any other person has provided valid property leads;
(5) the People's Court has, according to law, ruled to suspend enforcement against the judgment debtor subject to credit punishment due to trial supervision or bankruptcy proceedings;
(6) the People's Court has, according to law, ruled not to enforce;
(7) the People's Court has, according to law, ruled to terminate enforcement.
Where there is an inclusion period, the provisions of the preceding paragraph shall not apply. Within three working days after the expiration of the inclusion period, the People's Court shall delete the dishonest information.
After the dishonest information is deleted pursuant to paragraph 1 of this Article, if the judgment debtor falls under any of the circumstances provided in Article 1 of these Provisions, the People's Court may include it again in the list of judgment debtors subject to credit punishment.
Where, within six months after the deletion of dishonest information pursuant to Item (3) of paragraph 1 of this Article, the applicant for enforcement applies to include the judgment debtor in the list of judgment debtors subject to credit punishment, the People's Court shall not support the application.

Article 11: Where a citizen, legal person, or other organization that has been included in the list of judgment debtors subject to credit punishment considers that any of the following circumstances exists, it may apply to the enforcement court for correction:
(1) it should not have been included in the list of judgment debtors subject to credit punishment;
(2) the recorded and published dishonest information is inaccurate;
(3) the dishonest information should be deleted.
